24:56.800 --> 24:57.280
Not too bad.

24:57.280 --> 25:01.440
So that brings us to our final topic for this lecture.

25:03.520 --> 25:09.200
Which is the Chinese remainder theorem.

25:09.200 --> 25:19.720
And it's the Chinese remainder theorem because this thing has been found in, you know, relatively ancient Chinese literature.

25:19.720 --> 25:31.680
This kind of problem comes up when you try to solve, like, the joint motion of planets and stars and such.

25:31.680 --> 25:42.520
Because, basically, the problem of, you know, if you think about problems of time, there's a modularity there, right?

25:42.520 --> 25:51.640
Like, hours in the day, there's 24 hours in a day, so if we go 300 hours ahead, you know, if it's 5 o'clock right now and we go 300 hours ahead, what time is it?

25:52.200 --> 25:55.640
You can work that out, mod 24, if you're in military time, you know.

25:58.200 --> 26:03.500
And likewise, if you think about, like, the motion of planets and the sun and the moon and stuff.

26:03.500 --> 26:10.420
These all have different periods with respect to their orbit, you know, their orbits have particular periods.

26:10.620 --> 26:17.180
And so, this kind of problem would come up in astronomy or, worse yet, astrology.

26:17.480 --> 26:23.600
Alright, so, suppose you have mutually co-prime positive integers, n sub 1 through n sub k.

26:24.320 --> 26:32.400
And you're faced with a solution of, you're trying to find x, which is simultaneously the solution of these k congruences, alright?

26:32.400 --> 26:35.760
x congruent to a1, x congruent to a2, x congruent to a k.

26:36.320 --> 26:40.120
To make matters worse, each one of these has a different modulus, right?

26:40.160 --> 26:42.000
So, the moduli are n1 through nk.

26:42.880 --> 26:44.860
Well, the Chinese remainder theorem says, guess what?

26:44.900 --> 26:45.540
You can solve it.

26:45.960 --> 26:53.780
And the solution is a single congruence, which we can look at as mod n, where n is the product of all the moduli.

26:55.740 --> 27:01.040
And, so, you read something like this, and it seems like the proof is going to be scary.

27:01.040 --> 27:06.600
But, not only is the proof not scary, the proof is constructive in the sense that it tells you how to solve the problem.

27:07.360 --> 27:08.340
So, it's pretty neat.

27:10.140 --> 27:13.060
I really like the proof of the Chinese remainder theorem I'm about to show you here.

27:13.480 --> 27:16.000
It's a standard one, but here it is.

27:16.480 --> 27:19.280
So, I'm going to scooch this up a bit.

27:20.560 --> 27:21.240
There we go.

27:21.240 --> 27:29.900
So, define n, big n, to be the product of the moduli, n1 through nk.

27:30.100 --> 27:34.820
And define m, big m sub j, to be n divided by n sub j.

27:34.920 --> 27:38.560
So, in other words, it's the product of all the moduli except for n sub j.

27:38.740 --> 27:40.280
So, it skips over n sub j.

27:40.280 --> 27:50.440
So, immediately, you can notice that big m sub j is congruent to 0 mod n sub i for all i, except for the 1, equal to j.

27:51.240 --> 27:56.460
And, you can also notice that m sub j and n sub j are coprime.

27:56.460 --> 28:07.740
The gcd of big m sub j and little n sub j is 1, since n sub j has no common factor with any of the other moduli, right?

28:08.180 --> 28:10.540
Except 1, their coprime, right?

28:10.620 --> 28:11.580
No common factors.

28:12.720 --> 28:20.320
So, that means that the inverse of big m sub j exists in z mod nj.

28:21.100 --> 28:23.580
And, that's true for each j from 1 to k.

28:23.580 --> 28:33.720
In other words, you can pick an integer, d sub j, such that m sub j times d sub j is congruent to 1 mod n sub j, alright?

28:34.460 --> 28:40.080
And then, here's the neat thing, is you can build the solution by this formula.

28:40.320 --> 28:47.620
You just do x naught is a1, m1, d1, plus a2, m2, d2, plus da-da-da-da, ak, mk, dk.

28:47.620 --> 28:54.360
Okay, here the d's are coming from the inverse, finding the inverse of the big m sub j's.

28:55.040 --> 29:00.840
And, the m sub 1 through m sub k's are just defined up here, alright?

29:01.600 --> 29:03.900
And, the a's, they were given in the problem.

29:04.020 --> 29:07.180
Those are the coefficients appearing in the original equations right here, alright?

29:08.520 --> 29:10.900
Alright, let's see how that works.

29:11.300 --> 29:12.420
So, it's, alright.

29:12.420 --> 29:20.800
So, calculating modulo n sub j, since m sub i is congruent to 0 for i naught equal to j,

29:20.900 --> 29:26.900
this whole thing drops to just a sub j, m sub j, d sub j, right?

29:27.620 --> 29:33.680
But, that is congruent to a sub j, because m sub j, d sub j goes to 1, and it just leaves you with a sub j.

29:35.780 --> 29:40.040
So, x naught is congruent to a sub j, mod n sub j, which is what we want.

29:41.020 --> 29:44.440
Because, it holds for not just, this was done for arbitrary j.

29:45.180 --> 29:49.540
So, of course, it holds for j equals to 1 through k, which is the k congruences we faced.

29:51.040 --> 29:55.740
Alright, so the claim was that was given by a single congruence class, mod big n,

29:55.740 --> 29:58.460
where big n is the total, the product of the moduli.

29:59.660 --> 30:01.960
And, here's the proof that it's unique.

30:02.320 --> 30:07.380
So, suppose you have another simultaneous solution to all k of your congruences, right?

30:07.520 --> 30:12.580
y is congruent to a sub i, mod n sub i for all i equals to 1 to k.

30:14.040 --> 30:21.460
Then, that means that x naught is congruent to y for mod n sub i for all i equals 1 to k, right?

30:21.500 --> 30:23.520
Because, they're both congruent to a sub i.

30:23.520 --> 30:28.900
So, certainly, they're congruent to one another at each modulus.

30:29.560 --> 30:33.820
And, so what that means is that each modulus divides their difference.

30:34.980 --> 30:42.560
And then, remember that divisibility says that if you have n1 divides that, n2 divides that, n3 divides that,

30:42.560 --> 30:47.800
then, of course, n1 times n2 times n3 divides it as well.

30:48.880 --> 30:55.800
So, yeah, by basic divisibility, we get that the product of the moduli divides y minus x naught,

30:55.900 --> 31:01.020
but that's the same as saying that n divides y minus x naught,

31:01.100 --> 31:04.760
which is to say that x naught is congruent to y mod n,

31:04.760 --> 31:07.420
which is the uniqueness that we set out to prove.

31:08.420 --> 31:14.000
So, this proof gives a computational technique encapsulated by star.

31:14.140 --> 31:16.040
We'll use this in the examples that follow.

31:19.180 --> 31:20.140
Let's see here.

31:20.140 --> 31:27.060
All right, so example 9.

